      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B00.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 4.27
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B01",
      "act": "recap",
      "narration_text": "Last week, I looked at how significant an uncertainty is. This week, I focused on one important kind: uncertainty caused by gaps in our knowledge.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B01_Recap",
        "visual_intent": "Weeks 1–4 progression.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B01.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.92
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B02",
      "act": "literature review",
      "narration_text": "My fourth paper is This Is What We Don't Know: Treating Epistemic Uncertainty in Bayesian Networks for Risk Assessment, by Sahlin, Helle and Perepolkin, published in Integrated Environmental Assessment and Management in twenty twenty one.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B02_Paper",
        "visual_intent": "Citation card for paper 4.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B02.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 15.08
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B03",
      "act": "epistemic",
      "narration_text": "It focuses on epistemic uncertainty: uncertainty caused by limitations in our knowledge.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B03_Epistemic",
        "visual_intent": "What we know vs what we don't.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B03.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 5.82
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B04",
      "act": "Bayesian networks",
      "narration_text": "Environmental assessments often use Bayesian networks, which combine variables, scientific models, data, and expert judgment to estimate how likely different outcomes are.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B04_Network",
        "visual_intent": "A small Bayesian network.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B04.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 11.58
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B05",
      "act": "the gap",
      "narration_text": "But a probabilistic model doesn't automatically capture all the uncertainty. It may give a probability for an outcome, without revealing whether the data are weak, the structure is wrong, experts disagree, or key assumptions were made.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B05_Hidden",
        "visual_intent": "What one probability can hide.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B05.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 14.4
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B06",
      "act": "sources",
      "narration_text": "The authors identify where epistemic uncertainty can arise: in model structure, model parameters, expert knowledge, data, management scenarios, and the final assessment output.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B06_Sources",
        "visual_intent": "Six places epistemic uncertainty arises.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B06.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 12.01
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B07",
      "act": "distinction",
      "narration_text": "They stress the difference between uncertainty about the outcome itself, and uncertainty about the knowledge supporting that outcome.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B07_Layers",
        "visual_intent": "Outcome vs the knowledge beneath it.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B07.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 7.08
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B08",
      "act": "representation",
      "narration_text": "Different uncertainties may need different representations: probability distributions, intervals, alternative scenarios, or explicit caveats.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B08_Forms",
        "visual_intent": "Four ways to represent uncertainty.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B08.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.54
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B09",
      "act": "argument",
      "narration_text": "The central argument: risk assessments should be transparent about what is known, what is not known, and what limitations remain untreated.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B09_Transparent",
        "visual_intent": "Known · not known · untreated.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B09.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 8.36
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B10",
      "act": "insight",
      "narration_text": "For my research, this sets up a key distinction: a prediction may have a calculated probability, while the evidence behind it is still uncertain.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B10_Split",
        "visual_intent": "Prediction vs evidence.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B10.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.05
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B11",
      "act": "example",
      "narration_text": "For example, an A I might confidently conclude that a company is reducing its water footprint, based on a reported fifteen percent drop in water consumption.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B11_Water",
        "visual_intent": "Reported 15% drop → confident AI.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B11.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 9.43
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B12",
      "act": "example",
      "narration_text": "But the data may exclude some facilities, use estimated figures, or change reporting boundaries between years.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B12_Cracks",
        "visual_intent": "Three cracks in the evidence.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B12.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 7.0
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B13",
      "act": "insight",
      "narration_text": "The A I's conclusion and the strength of the evidence are two different things. So my research should assess at least two separate dimensions.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B13_Two",
        "visual_intent": "Two dimensions.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B13.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 7.66
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "B14",
      "act": "insight",
      "narration_text": "Conclusion confidence: how reliable is the A I's conclusion? And evidence reliability: how strong, complete, and credible is the information behind it? The two should not automatically be treated as the same.",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
      "shot": {
        "type": "MANIM",
        "source": "manim",
        "manim": "scenes.py::B14_Grid",
        "visual_intent": "Confidence × evidence grid.",
        "show": []
      },
      "audio_file": "mp3/beat-B14.mp3",
      "actual_duration_s": 13.57
    },
    {
      "beat_id": "BHTF",
      "act": "your turn",
      "narration_text": "Your turn. When an A I sounds confident, ask: how strong is the evidence underneath?",
      "voice": "af_bella",
      "engine": "kokoro",
      "voice_kokoro": "af_bella",
